// Tech CEOs — Series Six. Copy is final; art is generated from real photographs.
// Hand-curated (not auto-generated). `front`/`back` are written by the publish step —
// do not hand-edit those two fields.
//
// The brief: the people who ran the companies, ordered roughly by when they took charge.
// Every other set on this site cards the person who wrote the thing. This one cards the
// person who decided it would be built, priced, staffed and — the axis this site actually
// cares about — whether it would be given away.
//
// That last part is why a CEO set belongs here at all. Gerstner put a billion dollars
// behind Linux. McNealy opened Java and Solaris. Nadella bought GitHub and shipped a
// Linux kernel inside Windows. Huang’s proprietary driver is the reason Torvalds is on
// film insulting a graphics vendor, and the same company later open-sourced its GPU
// kernel modules. None of those were engineering decisions.
//
// Being admired is not the bar, and neither is being liked. Several people here are
// carded for decisions the open source world spent a decade fighting. The scouting
// reports say so plainly.
//
// On the house no-duplicates rule: it holds. Fifteen CEOs and founders are already carded
// in Series One to Five, so they are not repeated here — `alreadyCarded` below lists them
// and the roster page links them.
//
// Every scouting report is factual and checkable. No invented quotes: `note` is editorial
// voice, not attribution.

import type { RosterEntry, Rarity, Status, Source } from './roster.ts';
import { rarityLabel, statusLabel } from './roster.ts';

export type { Rarity, Status, Source };
export { rarityLabel, statusLabel };

export type Executive = RosterEntry & {
  /**
   * Skill stack for the card back, 0-100, scoped to this series. The axes the other sets
   * use do not travel: rating a chief executive on technical depth mostly measures how
   * long ago they stopped writing code. These rate the job itself. Judgements, not
   * sourced facts.
   *
   * product   — closeness to the thing: did they choose what got built, or only what got sold
   * operating — capital, hiring, supply chains, surviving the bad quarter
   * openness  — how much the company gave away under them: code, standards, patents, platform
   * influence — how much of the industry moved because of decisions they made
   */
  product: number;
  operating: number;
  openness: number;
  influence: number;
  note: string;
};

